{"meta": {"course": "cat100-supplement", "chapter": "03", "section": "01", "chapter_title": "Data Storytelling With Excel", "title": "Spreadsheet Structure and Clean Data", "description": "CAT 100 textbook section connecting spreadsheet structure and clean data to Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.", "learning_objectives": ["Analyze how the Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.", "Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.", "Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.", "Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it."], "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_data_storytelling_with_excel", "cat100_supplement_spreadsheet_structure_and_clean_data", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"], "estimated_time_minutes": 14, "order": 1, "top_tier_hardened": false, "prereq_section_ids": ["cat100-supplement/02/08"], "practice_ids": ["cat100_supplement_3_1_spreadsheet_structure_and_clean_data_artifact_signal", "cat100_supplement_3_1_spreadsheet_structure_and_clean_data_annotation_signal", "cat100_supplement_3_1_spreadsheet_structure_and_clean_data_ai_boundary", "cat100_supplement_3_1_spreadsheet_structure_and_clean_data_calibration"]}, "content": "# Spreadsheet Structure and Clean Data\n\n![Spreadsheet Structure and Clean Data textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/03-01-spreadsheet-structure-and-clean-data.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nEvery chart, pivot, and formula later in this module assumes the data underneath is clean. If the rows are not laid out the way Excel expects, nothing downstream behaves: a SUM skips numbers stored as text, a pivot splits `TX` and `texas` into two regions, and a chart inherits the mess. Clean structure is not housekeeping; it is the load-bearing step that decides whether the rest of your data story is trustworthy.\n\nA spreadsheet built for analysis follows one quiet rule called tidy data: one variable per column, one observation (record) per row, and exactly one value in each cell. Downloaded exports almost never arrive that way. They carry banner titles, merged cells, blank spacer rows, two facts crammed into one column, and three spellings of the same category. This section teaches you to spot those defects and repair them in a sequence a reader can re-trace against the original file.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nYou download a club's \"Q3 Sales Report\" and the first thing you see is a title in cell A1, an empty row 2, and a header row that does not start until row 3. The Region column reads `TX`, `texas`, and `Tx` in three different rows, and one name cell holds `Lopez, Maria`. You drop in `=SUM` to total the units and get a number that feels too low. Nothing is broken in an obvious way, yet the file cannot be analyzed as it stands.\n\nThat gap, between a file that looks finished and a table that is actually analyzable, is the real subject of this section. The fix is not cosmetic. Each repair changes what a formula or pivot will compute, so each repair is a decision you can defend.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nClean data has a precise definition you can check against any sheet. A table is analysis-ready when it satisfies three structural conditions and one consistency condition:\n\n| Rule | What it means | Common violation |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| One header row | Row 1 holds column names only, with nothing above it | A banner title in A1 or a blank spacer row 2 |\n| One value per cell | Each cell holds a single fact | `Lopez, Maria` (two facts) or `18 units` (number plus text) |\n| One record per row | Each row is one observation | A subtotal row mixed in with raw rows |\n| Consistent category labels | The same category is spelled one way | `TX`, `Tx`, and `texas` for one state |\n\nThe reason these rules matter is mechanical, not stylistic. Excel's aggregation tools (SUM, COUNT, pivot tables) treat each distinct text string as a distinct category and silently skip cells whose type is text when they expect a number. So `texas` and `TX` become two regions in a pivot, and a units value typed as `18 units` is read as text and excluded from a SUM. The visible symptom is a total that is lower than you expect with no error message to warn you. Cleaning the data is what makes the later count, sum, and chart say what you think they say.\n\nThree tools do most of the repair work: **Text to Columns** (split one cell into two on a delimiter such as a comma), **Find and Replace** (collapse spelling variants into one canonical label), and deleting non-data rows (the banner and the blank). Knowing which tool fixes which defect is the core skill here.\n\n## Worked Example\n\nSuppose a downloaded export arrives looking like this, with a banner title, a blank row, mixed name format, and inconsistent regions:\n\n```\n   A              B        C\n1  Q3 Sales Report\n2\n3  Name           Region   Units\n4  Lopez, Maria   TX       18\n5  Chen, David    texas     9\n6  Park, Sun      Tx       12\n```\n\nStep 1. Delete rows 1 and 2 so the header row `Name | Region | Units` becomes row 1 with no banner or blank row above it.\n\nStep 2. Split column A on the comma with Text to Columns so `Lopez, Maria` becomes `Lopez` in one column and `Maria` in the next. Now one value lives in one cell.\n\nStep 3. Standardize the Region column. Replace `texas` and `Tx` with `TX` so all three rows read `TX`. A pivot will now count one region, not three.\n\nStep 4. Confirm Units is numeric. `=SUM(units)` returns `39`. If any cell had been typed as `18 units`, that text cell would have been skipped and the total would read `21`, which is the visible symptom of the numbers-stored-as-text problem.\n\nThe clean table is now: three rows, one header, one value per cell, one spelling per region, and a SUM that matches the data. Each step is a decision a reader can re-trace against the raw export.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Repair the messy Q3 export, defect by defect\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"The export has a banner in A1 and a blank row 2 above the header. Before you compute anything, what is the first repair?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Run =SUM on the Units column to see the total\",\"Delete rows 1 and 2 so the header becomes row 1\",\"Make a bar chart of the three rows\",\"Rename the Region column\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Fix layout first. Delete rows 1 and 2 so Name | Region | Units sits in row 1 with data directly beneath it and no banner or spacer above.\"},{\"prompt\":\"The cell Lopez, Maria holds two facts. Which tool splits it into Lopez and Maria?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Find and Replace\",\"Text to Columns on the comma delimiter\",\"Delete the row\",\"SUM\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Text to Columns on the comma delimiter splits one cell into two, satisfying the one-value-per-cell rule.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Region reads TX, texas, and Tx in three rows. What collapses them to one label?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Text to Columns\",\"Find and Replace texas and Tx with TX\",\"Delete the duplicate rows\",\"AVERAGE\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Find and Replace collapses the spelling variants to a single canonical TX, so a pivot counts one region instead of three.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Finally confirm Units is numeric (right-aligned). =SUM now returns 39, matching a hand count. If a cell were typed 18 units, it would be skipped and the total would read 21, the visible symptom of numbers-stored-as-text.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\nBefore computing, the four repairs must run in a deliberate order. Drag them into the sequence a reviewer can re-trace.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Delete the banner and blank spacer rows so the header is row 1\",\"Split comma-joined cells with Text to Columns (one value per cell)\",\"Standardize category spellings with Find and Replace\",\"Confirm the Units column is numeric, then =SUM\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nClean structure before you compute anything. The order is deliberate: fix the layout, then split the cells, then standardize the labels, then confirm the numeric type, and only then sum, count, or chart. Running an analysis first and cleaning afterward means you never know which numbers were wrong.\n\nBefore you trust any total, run this three-point structural audit:\n\n1. **Layout check:** Is row 1 a pure header with no banner or blank row above it, and is every row below it one record?\n2. **Atomic check:** Does any cell hold two facts (a comma-joined name) or a number with a label (`18 units`)? If so, split it.\n3. **Type and label check:** Are the values you will sum actually numeric (right-aligned by default), and is each category spelled exactly one way?\n\nIf `=SUM` of a column you believe is numeric returns a total lower than a hand count, you have failed the type check: at least one value is text and is being skipped.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe most common error is treating a value typed as `18 units` or `$600` the same as a number. Excel left-aligns text and right-aligns numbers by default, so alignment is your fastest tell; a \"number\" hugging the left edge of its cell is text and will be dropped from sums.\n\nA second misreading is thinking that deleting the visible banner is enough. If a blank spacer row remains between the title and the data, a pivot table will treat the first data row as a header or stop reading at the gap. Remove the blank row too, so the header sits in row 1 with data directly beneath it.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the audit to a registrar export that lists course enrollments. The file opens with `Fall Enrollment Snapshot` in A1, a blank A2, and headers `Student, Course, Credits` starting in A3. The layout check fails twice, so delete rows 1 and 2 until `Student | Course | Credits` is row 1.\n\nScan for atomic violations. The Course column holds `CAT 100 - Intro`, two facts (a code and a title) in one cell. Use Text to Columns with the hyphen as the delimiter so the code and title live in separate columns; now you can pivot on the code alone. Then run the type check on Credits: a few cells read `3 cr`, which is text. Strip the `cr` so `=SUM(Credits)` counts every row instead of skipping the labeled ones.\n\nFinally, standardize labels. The Course code appears as `CAT 100`, `CAT100`, and `cat 100`. Find and Replace each variant with `CAT 100`. Only now is the snapshot ready to summarize, and every step is one a reviewer can re-run against the original download.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nA strong reader does not just notice that a sheet is messy; they predict the exact analytical failure each defect will cause. Train yourself to read a raw export and name the downstream consequence:\n\n1. A blank spacer row will truncate a pivot or break a SUM range.\n2. A comma-joined name cell cannot be sorted or filtered by last name.\n3. Three spellings of one region will appear as three rows in a pivot, inflating the category count.\n4. A number stored as text will silently lower every total it should be part of.\n\nWhen you can move from \"this looks untidy\" to \"this specific cell will drop \\$600 from the total,\" you are reading the structure, not just the surface.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A column you believe is numeric returns =SUM of 21, but a hand count of the rows totals 39. Which audit check just failed?\" explanation=\"A SUM lower than the hand count means at least one value is stored as text (left-aligned, like 18 units) and is being silently skipped. That is the type-and-label check, not a layout or atomic problem.\" options='[{\"text\":\"The layout check (a banner or blank row above the header)\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The type check (a value is stored as text and skipped by SUM)\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Nothing failed; 21 is simply the correct total\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_spreadsheet_structure_and_clean_data\", \"cat100_supplement_data_storytelling_with_excel\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nA strong cleaned-data artifact ships with a short repair log, not just the tidy table. The log names each defect found in the raw export, the tool used to fix it, and the proof that the fix worked. A model line reads: \"Region had `texas`, `Tx`, and `TX`; Find and Replace collapsed all three to `TX`; the pivot dropped from three region rows to one.\" The proof matters because it lets a reviewer confirm the repair without reopening the raw file.\n\nThe weakest version says only \"I cleaned the data.\" The strongest version lets a reader reconstruct the original mess and verify that the SUM now matches a hand count of the rows. Aim for the version where the before state, the action, and the numeric proof are all visible.\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nThe same audit transfers to a context with no banner and no blank rows: a survey export from a Google Form. Here the structural defects move into the cells. A \"select all that apply\" question stores `Pizza, Tacos, Sushi` in one cell, which violates the atomic rule and cannot be counted by option. A timestamp column mixes date and time, which you may need to split for a by-day pivot. The layout is fine, but the atomic and type checks still drive the work, so the skill is the audit itself rather than the specific banner-deleting steps from the worked example.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/03-01-spreadsheet-structure-and-clean-data-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"03.01\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title for CAT 100 03.01. A peer comment points to a evidence move issue connected to [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which trace would make the revision easiest to review?\" options='[\"A before-after artifact record linked to the decision you made and the evidence you used.\", \"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"0\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_spreadsheet_structure_and_clean_data\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that the Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title records audience, evidence source, AI/software support, accepted revision, rejected suggestion, and confidence change.\n\n## References\n\n- [NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)\n- [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students)\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- [WCAG accessibility](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/)\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "cat100_supplement_3_1_spreadsheet_structure_and_clean_data_artifact_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat100_supplement_spreadsheet_structure_and_clean_data", "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_spreadsheet_structure_and_clean_data", "cat100_supplement_data_storytelling_with_excel"], "stem": "You receive a sales export. Row 1 has the title 'Q3 Report', row 2 is blank, row 3 has the headers (Date, Region, Units), and the data starts in row 4. To build a clean tidy table that Excel features can use, what should you do first?", "options": ["Leave it as is because the title looks professional at the top.", "Delete rows 1 and 2 so the header row (Date, Region, Units) is row 1 with no blank rows above the data.", "Merge cells A1:C1 so the title spans the table.", "Color the header row so it stands out from the title."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "A tidy table needs one header row at the top with no title or blank rows above it, so Excel reads the column names correctly. The title belongs in a chart or a separate cell, not stacked above the data.", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_3_1_spreadsheet_structure_and_clean_data_title_in_header", "difficulty": "easy"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_3_1_spreadsheet_structure_and_clean_data_annotation_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat100_supplement_spreadsheet_structure_and_clean_data", "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_spreadsheet_structure_and_clean_data"], "stem": "Column C holds dollar amounts. Some cells show 1250, others show '$1,250.00', and one shows '1250 USD'. A SUM over the column returns far less than expected. What is the most likely cause?", "options": ["Excel cannot add more than 100 numbers at once.", "The cells containing '$1,250.00' and '1250 USD' are stored as text, so SUM ignores them.", "The SUM function only works on whole numbers.", "The column is too narrow to hold the values."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "When numbers are entered with currency symbols or unit words inside the cell, Excel may store them as text. Text values are skipped by SUM. Store the raw number and apply currency formatting through cell format instead.", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_3_1_spreadsheet_structure_and_clean_data_text_vs_number", "difficulty": "medium"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_3_1_spreadsheet_structure_and_clean_data_ai_boundary", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat100_supplement_spreadsheet_structure_and_clean_data", "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_spreadsheet_structure_and_clean_data", "ai_supported_learning"], "stem": "A roster has one column 'Name' with values like 'Lopez, Maria'. You need to sort by last name and also greet students by first name. What is the cleanest structural fix?", "options": ["Re-type every name by hand in the order you prefer.", "Split the one column into two columns, Last and First, using Text to Columns on the comma so each value lives in its own column.", "Sort the column alphabetically and assume last name comes first.", "Hide the Name column and add a note about the order."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "One value per cell is a core tidy-data rule. Text to Columns split on the comma gives a Last column and a First column, so each can be sorted or used independently.", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_3_1_spreadsheet_structure_and_clean_data_one_value_per_cell", "difficulty": "hard"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_3_1_spreadsheet_structure_and_clean_data_calibration", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat100_supplement_evidence_based_revision", "concept_ids": ["evidence_based_revision", "cat100_supplement_spreadsheet_structure_and_clean_data"], "stem": "Your Region column contains 'TX', 'Tx', 'texas', and 'Texas'. A pivot count treats these as four different regions. What is the right clean-up move before summarizing?", "options": ["Accept the four labels because they all refer to Texas anyway.", "Standardize the values to one consistent spelling and case (for example, all 'TX') so each region groups as one category.", "Add a footnote explaining the four versions mean the same thing.", "Sort the column so the versions sit next to each other."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Inconsistent category labels split one real group into several. Standardizing spelling and case before any pivot or chart keeps the counts honest.", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_3_1_spreadsheet_structure_and_clean_data_inconsistent_categories", "difficulty": "medium"}]}, "content_version": {"content_version": "34ae9902c64c3565ae379287401f4a78e38e539a6ee4ec98521984f6f070f320", "algorithm": "sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1", "parts": ["mdx", "meta", "practice", "misconceptions"]}, "misconceptions": [{"id": "cat100_supplement_3_1_spreadsheet_structure_and_clean_data_title_in_header", "pattern": "title_rows_above_data", "description": "Leaving title rows, blank rows, or banner text above the header row so the data range does not start cleanly", "trigger": "learner keeps a report title or blank row stacked on top of the header row", "feedback": "Put a single header row at the top with no title or blank rows above it. Move the title to a chart or a cell off to the side so features read the columns correctly.", "rail_action": "represent"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_3_1_spreadsheet_structure_and_clean_data_text_vs_number", "pattern": "numbers_stored_as_text", "description": "Typing currency symbols or unit words inside number cells so values are stored as text and skipped by SUM and AVERAGE", "trigger": "learner enters values like '$1,250.00' or '1250 USD' directly in the cell", "feedback": "Store the raw number and apply currency or unit formatting through cell format. Text values are ignored by math functions.", "rail_action": "explain"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_3_1_spreadsheet_structure_and_clean_data_one_value_per_cell", "pattern": "multiple_values_one_cell", "description": "Packing more than one fact into a single cell, such as last and first name together, so columns cannot be sorted or used independently", "trigger": "learner keeps combined values like 'Lopez, Maria' in one column", "feedback": "Split combined values so each cell holds one fact. Use Text to Columns so each piece can be sorted, filtered, or referenced on its own.", "rail_action": "practice"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_3_1_spreadsheet_structure_and_clean_data_inconsistent_categories", "pattern": "inconsistent_category_labels", "description": "Letting the same category appear under several spellings or cases so summaries split one group into many", "trigger": "learner summarizes a column with values like 'TX', 'Tx', 'texas', and 'Texas'", "feedback": "Standardize category labels to one spelling and case before any pivot or chart so each real group counts as one category.", "rail_action